N2  - Surgical resection remains the only hope for cure of pancreatic adenocarcinoma. However, 2/3 of the tumors are not resectable at the time of diagnosis due to distant metastatases or tumor extension to the celiac or mesenteric vessels. The current standard for pancreatic cancer staging is the use of multidetector computerized tomography (CT) scans with injection of contrast material. When non-resectability is due to locoregional extension, it is possible to perform preoperative radiotherapy and chemotherapy. This treatment could downstage the tumor and some expert teams may then propose a surgical resection, which becomes possible in 10 to 21% of the cases. However the results of this strategy are difficult to evaluate to due to a lack of clearly established criteria of non-resectability. Staging after radiotherapy is difficult as CT and echoendoscopy are not accurate in that setting. CA19-9 serum levels could help in assessing secondary resectability. Laparoscopy, with complete exploration of the abdomen, dissection of the previously involved vessels and multiple biopsies, remains therefore the only method to evaluate resectability after radiotherapy.
