N2  - Introduction: Pancreatic toxicity in patients treated with chemotherapy is usually considered as early toxicity in short time after chemotherapy. Some abnormalities are found during long follow-up after the end of chemotherapy. Objectives: To conduct a retrospective analysis of toxicity of breast cancer patients during chemotherapy Materials and Methods: We retrospectively have analyzed toxicity in 1643 patients treated for HER2-negative breast cancer. All patients received taxan-anthracycline-based chemotherapy regimens (paclitaxel 175 mg/m2 and doxorubicin 50 mg/m2 or docetaxel 75 mg/ m2 and doxorubicin 50 mg/m2), at 21 days intervals, for 4 cycles as neoadjuvant and 4 cycles as adjuvant. Mastectomies made between 4th and 5th cycles Results: The follow-up mean was 1 - 5 years after the end of chemotherapy. All patients had normal metabolic tests before chemotherapy. Acute pancreatitis was found in 9.1% patients during 8 cycles of chemotherapy. 5.2% patients had abdomen pain, metabolic abnormalities and ultrasound signs. Only metabolic abnormalities and ultrasound signs were found in 3.9%. During follow-up chronic pancreatitis was diagnosed in 20.6%. 50% patients with chronic pancreatitis manifested during follow-up period had had acute pancreatitis during neoadjuvant and adjuvant chemotherapy. Conclusion: This results show the need to control metabolic tests, to perform ultrasound scanning of abdomen and to adapt treatment according to the pancreatic function during chemotherapy.
