N2  - PURPOSE: Hepatoblastoma (HB) is the most common liver malignancy in children. Although liver transplantation in combination with other organs has been performed in adults with unresectable upper abdominal malignancies, there are no published reports of multivisceral transplantation (MVT) for HB. We report a case of HB requiring MVT to achieve gross tumor resection. METHOD: Case report; patient treated by a multidisciplinary transplant team. RESULTS: A 2-year-old male was diagnosed with stage 3 HB with signifcant vascular invasion extending from the portal vein down the superior mesenteric and splenic veins and into the orifce of the inferior mesenteric vein making him an unsuitable candidate for liver transplantation alone (Fig 1a). He was dependent on parenteral nutrition, likely due to mass effect of the tumor and severe portal hypertension. After 3 cycles of preoperative chemotherapy with cisplatin, vincristine, 5-fuorouracil, and doxorubicin, the patient underwent a MVT, with resection of native stomach, spleen, pancreas, liver, small bowel, and proximal colon. The MV graft consisted of stomach, liver, pancreas, and the entire small bowel. His initial alpha-fetoprotein was 893,915 ng/mL, but fell to 48 ng/mL one month post-transplant. Final pathology confrmed tumor in the mesenteric veins (Fig 1b). CONCLUSION: Patients with HB require complete gross resection to achieve a cure. Liver transplantation is now considered standard therapy for unresectable lesions. This unusual case required MVT to achieve a complete resection due to mesenteric vascular involvement. Additional follow-up will be required to determine the role of MVT in these cases.
