N2  - In the preoperative staging of esophagus/gastroesophageal cancer, FDG-PET demonstrates better diagnostic accuracy than CT and endoscopic sonography for the detection of distant metastases and for lymph node metastases that are not located in vicinity of the primary tumor. However, FDG-PET is not suitable for preoperative staging of locoregional lymph nodes. For the follow-up and restaging of these patients, FDG-PET is able to add important diagnostic information, except for the region of the anastomosis. A prospective phase II study shows evidence that a prognostically relevant therapy monitoring can be performed by FDG-PET as early as 2 weeks after initiation of neoadjuvant chemotherapy. Following the recommendations of the interdisciplinary PET consensus conference, restaging of patients with colorectal carcinoma represents a 1a-indication. Especially in patients with unclear CEA elevation, indeterminate imaging results and previous to surgery of metastases, PET helps to determine the optimal therapeutic strategy. Also sensitive detection of viable tumor tissue can be performed reliably by FDG-PET. It can be supposed that modern staging by combine PET-CT cameras will increasingly replace conventional restaging of patients. In selected cases of pancreas cancer, FDG-PET can help to differentiate chronic inflammation from tumor. Distant metastases and recurrence of disease can be detected by PET if therapeutically relevant. GIST accumulate FDG with high intensity. Therapy monitoring can be performed as early as one week after initiation of therapy. While most cholangiocellular cancer show good FDG accumulation hepatocellular cancers only are visualized in half of the cases. In special cases of primary liver tumor, FDG-PET delivers diagnostic information. © 2008 Dustri-Verlag Dr. Karl Feistle.
