N2  - It was carried out the adjuvant chemotherapy at 4 patients with carcinoma of the head and at 4 with carcinoma of the body of the pancreas. The number of the patients received chemotherapy was not allow to conclude about the effect of the adjuvant therapy. We tried to determine the effect theoretically. So, approximately 40,0% of our patients have had distant metastases. Taking into account that preoperatively the instrumental methods couldn't detect metastases smaller than 3-4 mm and intraoperatively surgeon could reveal only subcapsular micrometastases in the liver visually and by palpation; some of the patients without obvious metastases had micrometastases - occult metastases. Mathematically we calculated that the growing time for metastases with size from 3 mm to 3 cm was the same as for growth of micrometastases from 300 mcm into f 3 mm, and the same time was necessary for multiplying of 30 mcm cancer cell's group into 300 mcm micrometastases. Summarizing above indicated arguments we supposed that approximately 80% of patients with pancreatic cancer without obvious metastases to liver probably had occult micrometastases. This explained the reason of 5-year survival rate being lower 20% after radical resection for pancreatic cancer. The chemotherapy can demonstrate more effect in stages of micrometastases than in that of obvious ones. Taking into account the arguments indicated above, and the fact that chemotherapy increased the mean life time in patients with pancreatic cancer in our non-resected group, we can conclude that routine adjuvant chemotherapy theoretically may improve the outcome of patients undergoing radical surgery for pancreatic cancer.
