N2  - The history of gastric cancer surgery has taught us many lessons. The trend toward a more radical philosophy in the surgical treatment of gastric cancer emerged mainly around World War II in the United States. The limitation imposed by radical resections was attributed mostly to increased postoperative morbidity and mortality rates with marginal survival outcomes. The implementation of standardized surgical quality control measures by the Japanese created a standard surgical approach with defined nodal stations and a common nomenclature. The limitations imposed by retrospective data and the failure of two recent prospective trials have influenced Western surgeons' reluctance to pursue further investigation in the surgical merits of D2 lymphadenectomy in the presence of improved adjuvant chemo-radiation therapy regimens and now enhanced interest in the development of neoadjuvant protocols. These factors have changed the attitude of practicing surgeons to adopt a more cautionary and conservative approach in oncologic management of gastric cancer. © 2005 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
