N2  - PET is highly effective at detecting distant nodal involvement and organ metastases in primary cancers of the esophagus. It is equally effective in squamous cell carcinomas and in adenocarcinomas. Its sensitivity is low for assessing the local extent and proximal nodal status, but this can be achieved using EUS-fine-needle aspiration. The combination of both techniques is recommended for all patients potentially eligible for surgery. Such strategy is likely to be cost-effective. Some data indicate that PET performed after or during neoadjuvant treatment could predict survival. The real impact on the patient's management is not completely elucidated, however, and these treatments have yet to prove their value on the overall and disease-free survival rates.
