N2  - The period from 1991 to 2003 at Johns Hopkins Hospital was seminal in the development and maturation of the pancreatic cancer program. As part of this process a clinical team consisting of surgeons, medical oncologists, pathologists, radiation oncologists, and research nurses was formed. This group developed a series of postoperative adjuvant studies involving chemotherapy and radiotherapy. These studies have demonstrated that increasingly intense multiagent chemotherapy can be combined with radiotherapy in this domain with acceptable toxicity. The authors' efforts with standard therapy in the early 1990s convinced them of the definite but limited value of single-agent 5-FU and irradiation. Preliminary results with more intense 5-FU and mitomycin C-based regimens have been studied; final analysis is pending. Secondarily the authors have become impressed with the need to integrate consideration of known prognostic factors carefully in the design of trials and in the analysis of their outcomes. Finally, the authors remain convinced that radiotherapy has an integral role in the adjuvant therapy of periampullary adenocarcinoma in spite of recent controversy on this point.
