N2  - Approximately seven percent of clinically apparent ovarian malignancies are neoplasms metastatic to the ovary. These metastases are most commonly derived from the reproductive tract, gastrointestinal tract or breast. Accurate identification of metastatic ovarian neoplasms is important because the prognosis and treatment of metastatic neoplasms varies substantially from primary ovarian malignancies. Most women with primary or metastatic ovarian malignancies exhibit presenting symptoms, although they are often identified in retrospect. Although there is much overlap in symptomatology, women with secondary ovarian malignancies tend to present with abdominal pain, whereas women with primary ovarian neoplasia tend to present with bloating and increasing abdominal girth. Because gastrointestinal cancers are part of cancer family syndromes such as HNPCC and Lynch 2, patients with an extensive family history of colorectal, endometrial, breast, ovarian, pancreatic or gastric cancers should be referred for genetic counseling and testing where appropriate. Physical examination findings rarely accurately identify the primary site because metastases to the ovary tend to be larger than the primary tumor. All patients who have not had recent colorectal screening should have a rectal examination and stool guaiac test performed during the preoperative evaluation. Computed tomography and pelvic ultrasound are insensitive at diagnosis of GI tract malignancies. Because liver metastases are infrequently seen in primary ovarian malignancies, gastrointestinal carcinomas should be included in the differential diagnosis when liver metastases are identified. At laparotomy, metastases to the ovary are typically bilateral, with a smooth external surface, often with multiple nodules within the ovaries. Frozen section is usually accurate, but less so in neoplasms of mucinous and endometrioid histology. The surgeon should aim to provide a complete resection if this is feasible without undue morbidity. If residual disease must be left in place, careful documentation of the sites and volume remaining are helpful for future consultation regarding investigational treatments. Aggressive cytoreduction including peritonectomy followed by intraoperative hyperthermic intraperitoneal chemotherapy has been advocated for treatment of metastatic GI carcinomas. Although most of the data regarding this modality is retrospective, a randomized controlled trial has shown a survival advantage to this approach compared with conservative surgery and weekly 5-FU chemotherapy, albeit with significant toxicity. New chemotherapeutic agents, such as oxaliplatin, irinotecan and bevacizumab have also improved the response rate and survival compared with 5-FU alone for patients with metastatic GI carcinomas. Hopefully, as new treatments develop, a more optimistic prognosis may be offered to women diagnosed with secondary ovarian neoplasia.
