N2  - Surgery remains the standard treatment in biopsy-proven gastric cancer except of well-selected early tumors (T1a, mucosal type) in experienced hands. In these cases endoscopic mucosal resection might be of equal efficacy. However, there is a lack of study data with adequate follow-up. For all other tumors gastrectomy with D2 lymphadenectomy should be favored if it can be performed safely and with limited morbidity. Splenectomy including resection of the pancreatic tail is the main contributor to complications and postoperative mortality. Thus, dissection of lymph nodes along the pancreas and at the splenic hilum should only be performed if they are at risk for metastases. To accomplish this, sentinel node mapping might be the tool for the future, whereas the Maruyama computer program is of statistical help today. New cytostatic drugs still need to prove efficacy in the adjuvant setting and there is no general indication for adjuvant chemotherapy outside of prospective, randomized trials. Even the contribution of postoperative radiochemotherapy (RCT) according to the US protocol INT0116 needs to be thoroughly discussed prior to establishing it as a standard. A prospective randomized study comparing D2 gastrectomy +/- RCT is urgently required. Regarding neoadjuvant treatment concepts, the MAGIC trial has shown superior efficacy of combined modality therapy, and the results of similar trials hopefully will confirm these findings.
