N2  - Conventional imaging modalities are considered in many institutions to be the initial method of choice for the workup of GI diseases, because of their cost-effectiveness and ease of performance. Before the emergence of clinical whole-body PET, the diagnostic workup for staging and restaging of patients with GI tract malignancies consisted of anatomic imaging modalities such as CT, US, and colonoscopy. These techniques are limited in their ability to discriminate reliably between malignant tumor and benign lesions (i.e., differentiating postsurgical anatomic alterations and scarring after radiation from residual or recurrent disease). Accurate staging of GI tract malignancies is important to determine the best therapeutic approach for individual patients. FDG-PET provides important diagnostic information. In particular, a focal increase of FDG uptake seems to be a reason-ably specific sign of malignant tumor. PET-FDG is also indicated for the preoperative staging of GI tumors that appear to be resectable by CT scanning and to clarify any equivocal abnormalities that are present on CT scans. Whole-body PET-FDG imaging has become a useful tool for the detection of most recurrent GI tract malignancies and for determining which patients will benefit from surgery. PET-FDG imaging also seems to be useful in the clarifi-cation of CT-occult metastatic disease, allowing nontherapeutic resection to be avoided. The results of PET-FDG imaging are complementary to other imaging techniques and correlation is essential. Copyright © 2002 by the American Society for Gastrointestinal Endoscopy.
