N2  - The prognosis of pancreatic adenocarcinoma remains poor, with a 5-year survival rate lower than 5%. Resection, the gold standard treatment, can be performed in less than 10% of patients. Following surgery, the median survival is 12 months. Concomitant chemoradiation, as an adjuvant treatment could be superior to surgery alone, in terms of survival; controlled trials are currently performed. Neoadjuvant chemoradiation is a new approach, potentially able to increase survival and resection rate. Finally, current data regarding intraoperative irradiation are exposed. (C) 2000 Editions scientifiques et medicales Elsevier SAS.
