N2  - Since the first successful gastric resection by Billroth in 1881, much research has been conducted in order to evaluate results and improve outcomes of this operation. Fortunately results have indeed improved. Still, for each patient the risk-benefit ratio should be known to have an optimal effect on the procedure. The extension of the disease, the operative procedure, and the selection of the patient, of course, play a crucial role in this. The JRSCG has provided guidelines for the standardization of surgical treatment and pathologic evaluation? Thanks to these guidelines we currently speak the same language concerning gastric cancer, and the possibility has been created to compare different studies on this subject. Nevertheless, results in gastric cancer treatment show a great deal of variation. Several surgical, patient, tumor, and treatment-related factors may play a role in this. Resectability of gastric cancer has increased in the past decades, not only because of earlier detection but also because of the technical ability to perform extended operations. Pessimism about outcomes, however, may prevail and lead to negative attitudes, which may become self-fulfilling attitudes. If the surgeon performs palliative procedures because of a believe that gastric cancer is nearly always incurable at presentation, this low expectation will be fulfilled. Conversely, inappropriate aggressive surgery in unfit patients may lead to increased postoperative morbidity and postoperative mortality without improving long-term outcome. Tumor stage is an important prognostic factor in gastric cancer. There is a strong relationship between depth of invasion and the occurrence of lymph node metastasis, and the occurrence of lymph node metastasis is strongly related to 5-year survival. Because the incidence of lymph node metastasis is known to be greater in the node stations near the tumor than in more distant ones and the distance between tumor and lymph node shows a direct relationship with survival, the question remains which patients benefit from an extended lymph node dissection. In Japan, where extended (D2) dissections are standardized, results from gastric cancer treatment are encouraging. In Western countries in nonrandomized trials there seems to be a better survival rate for patients with a D2 dissection, although the Japanese results could not be matched. Two large randomized Western studies, the DGCT and the MRC trial, have recently compared D1 and D2 dissections and final results are available. These studies show that morbidity and hospital mortality are higher for the D2 dissection group without a significantly better survival. These studies do not generally support the standard use of extended (D2) lymph node dissection in Western patients with gastric cancer. With a mean survival rate of 45% at 5 years, the results of the Dutch D1-D2 trial for either arm are by far better then the previous experience in Western countries. This indicates the benefit of trial participation with optimal selection, use of surgical techniques, pathology compliance, and postoperative care throughout the study. Subtotal versus total gastrectomies for tumors in the distal part of the stomach often have been a point of discussion. From previous studies it seems that a subtotal gastrectomy has less morbidity and the same survival rate, provided that an adequate tumor-free margin can be obtained. Resection line involvement should be evaluated perioperatively by frozen section examination. Resection of spleen and pancreas in all studies leads to an increased morbidity and hospital mortality, eventually leading to a decreased survival. Therefore, in Western countries resection of pancreas and spleen is only recommended if tumor ingrowth prohibits a possible curative resection. Age is found to be an important prognostic factor in Western patients. Although the resectability and curability rates do not differ among age groups, there is a significantly increased morbidity and mortality rate and a significantly worse survival rate with increasing age. The presence of a positive lymph node station 16 (N4) or a positive cytology of abdominal fluid is related to poor prognosis. Consequently, extended resections should be avoided. The technical skills of the surgeon may be of influence as well on outcomes in gastric surgery. Only in the German Gastric Cancer Study was a significant difference found between surgeons with more or less experience, especially with regard to anastomotic leakage. Other trials could not show a significant difference. Although morbidity in Western countries exceeds Japanese morbidity, Japanese surgeons operating on Western patients show no significant difference in morbidity and hospital mortality when compared to Western surgeons. Therefore, the difference between Japanese and Western studies seems not to be caused by the skills of their surgeons. Nevertheless, a learning curve for extended resections has been shown by McCulloch. This may be one of the reasons that more experienced hospitals show a lower morbidity and hospital mortality. Other reasons for better results may be better patient selection, better pathologic analysis (stage migration phenomenon), better preoperative staging, and the possibility for (neo) adjuvant chemotherapy. In view of the much lower postoperative mortality rate reported by specialized centers, the treatment of gastric cancer should be the territory of a multidisciplinary team of committed specialists in all areas to provide optimal results. Preoperative assessment of the lesion and the patient and perioperative management are as important as the operation itself.
