N2  - The prognosis for carcinomas of the exocrine pancreas is worse than for any other epidemiologically relevant carcinoma and survival times are very short. Only 1-4% of affected persons survive longer than five years. Pancreatic carcinomas grow aggressively, spreading out in early stages along peripancreatic neurons and lymphatics. These tumors are usually diagnosed too late and display only limited response to radiation and chemotherapy treatment. Only a few patients with small localized cancers may undergo curative surgery, but, face an uncertain prognosis. The proper use of adjuvant and neoadjuvant treatment still has to be established. Endoscopic and surgical bypass and drainage procedures are appropriate for locally advanced tumors. Patients with good performance status may benefit from combined modality (radiochemotherapy) treatment. Median survival in metastatic carcinoma of the pancreas is four months and may be prolonged to some eight months by chemotherapy. Major problems in advanced stages are gastric outlet stenosis, bile duct occlusion, severe abdominal pain and the anorexia-cachexia syndrome. Therefore supportive care is of primary importance in these cases. Dedicated palliative care needs to include the confrontation of psychological and social problems, but may then help patients cope with the enormous burden of the rapidly advancing disease. Improving this extremely unsatisfactory therapeutic situation should include the recommendation to treat as many patients as possible, in phase I and II studies with innovative new therapies.
