N2  - Carcinomas of the esophagus, stomach and pancreas are invasive tumors with bad prognosis. In Poland the overall 5-year survival rate for esophageal, gastric and pancreatic carcinoma is 5%, 25%, and 4%, respectively. Surgery is considered as a gold standard for both curative and palliative intents. But resectability/operability is not satisfactory and in Poland the rate for esophageal, gastric and pancreatic carcinoma is 20%, 50- 70% and less than 20%, respectively. Therefore in order to improve the surgical results current standard of treatment of advanced esophageal, gastric and pancreatic carcinoma constitutes combined modality approach associating surgery with chemotherapy and radiotherapy in different combinations, as neo-adjuvant - preoperative therapy for resectable/operable cases, as inducing modality aimed at tumor downstaging in unresectable, locally advanced cases, and as an adjuvant - postoperatively in cases after optimal surgery at risk for local recurrence (indication for radiotherapy) and for systemic spread (indication for chemotherapy). In stage II-III esophageal carcinomas the current trend is focused on preoperative programs (neo-adjuvant radio- and/or chemotherapy using mainly the drugs 5- Fluorouracyl and Cisplatinum) or definitive chemo-radiotherapy with high dose rate (HDR) brachytherapy (interstitial therapy) as an alternative for surgery. In stage IV esophageal carcinoma (metastatic disease) when pallation is only feasible, at first chemo-radiotherapy is taken into consideration, secondly external beam radiotherapy and thirdly HDR brachtherapy. Other forms of palliative treatment which are intubation, bouginage, laser vaporization or argon plasma coagulation can be used in association with oncological modalities or primarily in low performance status patients. In gastric cancer the optimal set of chemotherapeutic drugs in investigated in pallative patients (measurable metastatic lesions) using high dose (2-3 g/m2 body surface) of 5-Fluorouracyl in combination with Folinic acid (Leucovorin) and Cisplatinum. Radiotherapy is considered only postoperatively for pT3, pN2 and/or positive surgical margin patients (resection R1 - microscopically non- radical) who are not fit for further surgery. In pancreatic cancer the optimal set of chemotherapeutic drugs is also investigated in palliative patients using high doses (2 6 g/m2 body surface) of 5-Fluorouracyl and Folinic acid Gemyctabine is considered as a new prospective drug for both the first and the second line palliative therapy. In unresectable cases (locally advanced disease) HDR brachytherapy combined with chemo-radiotherapy is investigated prospectively.
