N2  - Background: The anatomical position of the stomach between pancreas, spleen, transverse colon, diaphragm, abdominal wall, supra renal glands, small bowel and retroperitoneum, explains the possible infiltration of these structures in advanced carcinoma of the stomach. Methods: Analysis of patients operated on gastric cancer between 1982 to 1997 at the Department of Surgery of the Technical University Munich. 170 patients were primarily resected with gastric carcinoma pT4, 21 patients were resected after neoadjuvant chemotherapy with uT4 carcinoma. Results: RO resections after surgical treatment alone occurred in 35.9% and in 76% of patients after neoadjuvant chemotherapy with uT4 tumors. Postoperative morbidity was 38.2% and 15%, lethality 5.9% and 0% after primary surgery and neoadjuvant chemotherapy followed by resection, respectively. 5-year overall survival for primarily surgically treated patients was 7.6% and 40% after neoadjuvant chemotherapy. Conclusions: In comparison to surgery alone in pT4 carcinoma new successful therapeutic strategies are developing. However, due to the small numbers of cases and the short observation time preliminary conclusions should be avoided. The presented results need to be evaluated in a randomised prospective study.
