N2  - Only minor achievements have been made in the systemic chemotherapy of advanced pancreatic cancer, when compared to the so-called standard, 5-FU or supportive therapy. Systemic chemotherapy with the FAM-protocol induced a significant survival advantage vs untreated controls. Gemcitabine, when compared to 5-FU monotherapy, let to a longer survival and better palliation. Phase II-studies with 5-FU modulation protocols (Folinic acid + Interferon- α or Dipyridamol + Folinic acid + Mitomycin C) resulted in median survival times of 10 and 15 months, respectively. In nonresectable nonmetastasized advanced pancreatic cancer radiochemotherapy was associated with response rates of 48 - 88% and median survival times of 8 - 14 months. The survival times in radiochemotherapy were significantly prolonged, when compared to radiotherapy or chemotherapy alone. The response rates to regional chemotherapy ranged between 21% and 69% and the median survival times from 7.5-14 months. Only prospective randomized controlled studies can prove the assumed survival advantage in regional chemotherapy. In adjuvant postoperative treatment, radiochemotherapy, as shown in the GITSG study, improved the median survival time to 18 - 21 months, and the 5- and 10-year survival rates. This significant effect was also seen in historical control studies. Several studies applying either preoperative radiochemotherapy, systemic or regional chemotherapy provided evidence, that tumors may be downstaged leading to secondary resectability. In spite of this potential advantage of those neoadjuvant treatments, the survival advantage achievable by neoadjuvant treatment of all resectable and primarily nonresectable tumors must be proven in controlled trials. Our current experimental clinical concept consists of combining celiac artery infusion and radiotherapy for nonresectable UICC ≤ III and resected tumors within prospective studies, aiming at developing prospective controlled randomized trials. Regarding the actual literature results and our own experience, metastasized pancreatic cancer should be treated with 5-FU, Gemcitabine or the combination of 5-FU + Folinic acid + Interferon-α. Locally advanced pancreatic cancers without distant metastases, depending on the experience of the hospital, should receive either radiochemotherapy or regional chemotherapy. In the same sense, postoperative adjuvant treatment options are radiochemotherapy or regional chemotherapy. The validity of those treatment modalities should be confirmed in further controlled studies, before they can be recommended as standard.
