N2  - Tumors in the liver mostly represent metastases from gastrointestinal tumors, in particular: colorectal cancer, pancreatic and gastric cancer as well as carcinoids and neuroendocrinologically active tumors. Primary liver cancer is relatively rare in countries nonendemic for hepatitis B. Liver metastases from colorectal cancer and neuroendocrine tumors often exhibit a specific biology with a particular preference to grow only in the liver without rapid and/or massive extrahepatic spread. Therefore, locoregional treatment modalities for these metastases as well as for primary liver cancer might be the treatment of choice. Whereas liver metastases from carcinoids and neuroendocrine tumors are highly sensitive to locoregional treatment, in particular chemoembolization using adriamycin and starch microspheres or gel foam, liver metastases from colorectal cancer are highly sensitive to interarterial chemotherapy with 5-FU plus folinic acid, mitomycin and experimental agents such as Oxaliplatin or Irinotecan. A meta-analysis demonstrated that locoregional treatment results in a higher response rate and a borderline better survival than systemic chemotherapy in colorectal cancer; however, recent developments in systemic combination chemotherapy including 5-FU/folinic acid/Oxaliplatin or 5-FU/folinic acid/CPT-11 have demonstrated a high response rate of about 60%. Therefore, at present, the treatment of choice for liver metastases of colorectal cancer is systemic chemotherapy, followed only in case of insufficient response or progression under systemic chemotherapy, by a locoregional approach. However, the good response rate under the 3rd-generation protocols for metastatic colorectal cancer opens the avenue for a combined modality approach with preoperative systemic chemotherapy for borderline resectable or irresectable liver metastases, followed by resection in case of response. This approach allows a prolonged or even a long-term survival in about one third of the patients undergoing this procedure and should be evaluated in every patient. The liver metastases are an excellent human in-vivo model for the investigation and application of new agents and modalities including combination chemotherapies, tumor necrosis factors with hyperthermia, cytokines or gene therapeutic approaches with application of pro-drugs or apoptosis regulators specifically into the tumor cells, driven by tumor-antigene-specific promoters, e.g. for CEA.
