N2  - Aims of neoadjuvant therapy are to increase the rate of curative surgical procedures (RO), to improve long-term survival and to better assess tumoral response to radiochemotherapy. In stage II and IIIA esophageal cancer, neoadjuvant radiochemotherapy allows interesting local responses. In stage II gastric cancer, the RO resection rate increases after neoadjuvant chemotherapy. No effect of neoadjuvant therapy has been demonstrated so far in pancreatic cancer. Preoperative radiotherapy associated with sensibilization chemotherapy is the new therapeutic standard of T3 and T4 rectal tumors, and allows a reduction in the abdominoperineal resection gate. Negative effects to neoadjuvant therapy on surgery appear to be modest. The patient benefits of an oncological treatment set up by multidisciplinary team.
