N2  - Background: The surgical treatment of pancreatic cancer has been considerably improved in specialized centers with respect to perioperative mortality and long term survival. Nonetheless resectability rates above 40% can not be achieved even in these centers and approximately 3 of 4 radically treated patients die within 5 years owing to local recurrence and peritoneal or liver metastases. The necessity for adjuvant therapeutical measures is thus obvious and can not be doubted. Methods: This paper discusses adjuvant and neoadjuvant treatment strategies and makes an attempt to stress the most promising therapeutical approaches. Results: Randomized controlled trials with the administration of chemo- and/or radiotherapy after radical surgery are rare, the palliative indication has been investigated predominantly and so far these results have been discouraging. Conclusions: The adjuvant chemo- or radiotherapeutic treatment of pancreatic cancer patients can not be demanded as a routine measure. The individual decision for adjuvant treatment is within the discretion of the treating institution. According to the authors' opinion adjuvant chemotherapy and radiotherapy should be subject to further investigation in clinical trials.
