N2  - Background: The insufficient long-term results, even after radical operation of malignant pancreatic tumors, force the development of new multimodal therapeutic strategies. Methods: The results of adjuvant and neoadjuvant chemotherapy have been reviewed in context with the clinical results of palliative therapeutic protocols in advanced pancreatic cancer (adenocarcinoma). Results: With respect to the biology of ductal pancreatic cancer the postoperative radiochemotherapy seems to be the most effective and established procedure. With postoperative radiochemotherapy, the Gastrointestinal Tumor Study Group could extend median survival times of untreated controls from 11 to 18 to 20 months in treated patients. These results were reproduced in non-randomizing trials concerning the length of surivival time (Mayo Clinic). Preoperative radiochemotherapy might lead to a downstaging of the tumour and an increase of resectability; an extend of survival is not assured. Systemic adjuvant chemotherapy with the FAM-Protocol increased survival in one randomized trial. However, this trial also included patients with papillary cancers. 2 groups were able to provide evidence that adjuvants regional chemotherapy might increase survival from 9.3 to 21 months or 18 to >24 months respectively, when treated versus historical control patients were compared. Conclusions: The combination of radiotherapy and celiac artery infusion seems to be the most promising strategy to treat either locally advanced non-resectable or resected pancreatic cancer.
