N2  - Today approximately a dozen syndromes of neuroendocrine tumors of the pancreas are identified. Functionally inactive tumors account for 18-36% of all endocrine tumors of the pancreas. With the exception of insulinomas, the frequency rate of malignancy is more than 50%. The exclusion of multiple endocrine neoplasia type I is important because of diagnostic and therapeutic implications. The histogenesis of the endocrine cells of the gastroendopancreatic system resebles more the diffuse neuroendocrine system than the classic APUD-concept. The most frequent endocrine tumors of the pancreas are insulinomas (about 50%) and gastrinomas (about 25%). In case of a primary operation without a multiple endocrine neoplasia type I syndrome, a preoperative localization procedure is not necessary due to the high sensitivity of the intraoperative exploration. The cure rate by means of surgical treatment for insulinomas is more than 95%. Curative resection for gastrinomas is only 30-40%. Therapeutical options for liver metastases are tumor resection, chemoembolization, antihormone treatment, chemotherapy, and liver transplantation.
