N2  - Despite improved surgical treatment resulting in reduced perioperative mortality, the survival rate of pancreas carcinoma patients is disappointing. New methods of dose planning and radiation technique enable the application of a sufficient dose without exceeding the tolerance in neighbouring critical organs. Radiochemotherapy with concomitant application of cytostatics (5-fluorouracil, mitomycin C) enhances the efficiency on the tumour. The percentage of resectable pancreas tumours is raised by neoadjuvant radiotherapy and radiochemotherapy. At present, the most effective procedure is dose escalation in regions of high risk of relapse by intraoperative radiation therapy or by afterloading treatment in combination with percutaneous radiochemotherapy. The adjuvant therapeutic methods improve the rate of local tumour control, prolong the median survival time and reduce clinical symptoms. It is dubious whether adjuvant therapy may positively influence the long-time survival of pancreas carcinoma patients because patients' death is based on liver metastasis and tumour generalization in this period. More aggressive adjuvant methods should not restrict the possibilities of surgical therapy.
