N2  - A 61-year-old man was admitted to our hospital in January 1996, suffuring from anorexia and body weight loss. The upper G I series and endoscopic examination revealed a type 3 cancer in the posterior wall of the cardia. The CT scan showed the enlargement of the many No. 16 lymph node. Furthermore, the IVC was diagnosed to be involved by the metastic No. 16 lymphnode. Neoadjuvant chemotherapy (NAC) was planed in order to decrease or disappear the tumor. Two cycles of FLP therapy were done. After chemotherapy, a CT scan showed marked shrinking of the No. 16 lymph nodes and the IVC became free from cancer invasion. Resection was performed of the whole stomach, the body and tail of the pancreas, the spleen and the gallbladder. Histological findings demonstrated the degeneration of cancer cells, infiltration of the foamy cells formation of the granulation, necrosis of the tissue, and fibrosis in the primary tumor and metastatic regional lymph nodes. There were no viable cancer cells in the No. 16 lymph nodes. The histological changes against NAC were judged grade 2. The NAC of FLP was so effective that could induce downstaging from the stage IV b to the stage III be. FLP therapy: CDDP 75 mg drip i. v, on days 1 and 8,5 FU 500 mg/24 hours i. v. continuous infusion daily from 1 through day 8 and Leucovorian 30 mg i. v. daily from day 1 through day 8.
