N2  - Intraoperative radiation therapy for pancreatic cancer is a safe procedure in the hands of an experienced team of surgeons and radiotherapists. Severe delayed side effects or complications are rare. In comparison with curative or palliative surgical approaches alone local tumor control is improved by intraoperative radiation therapy (IORT). Median survival is not or only insignificantly prolonged by IORT compared with palliative surgical procedures alone, in contrast to curative operations alone, where slight improvement of median survival time is achieved by additional IORT or percutaneous irradiation. The combination of IORT and percutaneous irradiation clearly increases the local tumor control and shows a tendency for improved median survival times. Long term survivors are rare in all subgroups of patients, and surgical procedures remain the most important approach with respect to survival times. For patients with nonresectable pancreatic cancer a prompt and highly impressive pain relief is the most important palliative effect for a period of about 4-5 months. Occasionally this effect may be exploited also for patients with distant metastases. A stabilization of Karnofsky's s index may also be achieved by IORT, particularly in patients with nonresectable nonmetastasized tumors. Generally the poor prognosis of pancreatic cancer remains unchanged even if IORT is included in multimodal treatment concepts. Implementation of IORT in a stage-adapted procedure of preoperative radiochemotherapy, operation and IORT is an attractive therapeutic approach which is currently being evaluated. It combines the possibly highest therapeutic effectiveness with avoidance of excessive aggressiveness in the treatment of pancreatic cancer.
