N2  - MOST PATIENTS with pancreatic cancer are seen with jaundice and/or pain and a pancreatic mass demonstrated by computed tomography. A careful clinical examination, as well as judicious application of endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography and endoscopic ultrasound, will exclude the majority of patients with benign conditions and those with nonpancreatic periampullary tumors. Once the diagnosis of carcinoma of the pancreas is entertained, further preoperative staging is warranted to (1) avoid unnecessary surgery in patients with metastatic or locally advanced disease, (2) limit radiation treatment to those without metastatic disease, and (3) identify potentially resectable patients for safe pancreatectomy and possible neoadjuvant treatment protocols. Laparoscopy was used to identify intraabdominal metastases in 24% of 114 patients, and angiography demonstrated vascular involvement in 48% of those without distant metastases. Peritoneal cytology was positive in 17% of patients; its significance in patients who are otherwise resectable is not known.
