N2  - In 1955, Zollinger and Ellison first described a syndrome characterized by a severe, often fatal form of peptic ulcer disease accompanied by marked hypersecretion of gastric acid and non-B-islet cell tumors (gastrinomas) of the pancreas. There are two distinct problems in patients with Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, the peptic ulcer diathesis and the malignant tumor. Historically, the severe peptic ulcer disease in these patients was treatable only with total gastrectomy, usually under emergency conditions. However, the advent of the histamine H2-receptor antagonists and more recently, the H+K+ -ATPhase inhibitor, omeprazole, has increased the ability to control gastric acid hypersecretion medically, obviating the need for total gastrectomy and allowing time to determine the location and extent of the gastrinoma. Currently, gastric acid hypersecretion can be controlled medically in the long-term in almost every patient, and with the earlier diagnosis these patients are now less likely to succumb to the complications of peptic ulcer disease. Instead, a number of recent results suggest that the growth and possible metastatic potential of the gastrinoma will become the major determinant of long-term survival in these patients and that increasing emphasis directed at management of the gastrinoma might increase long-term survival. First, whereas the original studies suggested that gastrinomas are slow growing and indolent in behavior, more recent long-term studies of patients with gastrinoma who survived total gastrectomy and subsequently died, show that at least 50% of the deaths were due to tumor progression. Second, it has become increasingly evident that neither total gastrectomy as claimed by some nor medical therapy prevent tumor progression. Third, in the original studies only 5% of patients with gastrinoma were surgically cured. However, because of the better definition of which patients may benefit from surgery, it is currently estimated that between 20 and 40% of patients with sporadic nonfamilial gastrinoma may be treated successfully by tumor resection, obviating the need for either gastric resection or lifelong drug therapy. Fourth, with the availability of sophisticated and increasingly sensitive techniques for preoperative localization of the tumor, it may be feasible to detect tumors at an early stage and resect them successfully with potential cure in an increasing number of patients. In a number of recent reports the advances in the medical and surgical management of the gastric acid hypersecretion in patients with Zollinger-Ellison syndrome have been reviewed, In this article advances in the management of the gastrinoma are reviewed.
