N2  - The clinical use of deep local and regional hyperthermia as an adjunct to radiation therapy should be considered in situations in which a) the probability of palliation or local-regional control is low by currently available standard treatment modalities and b) hyperthermia can be safely administered to the tumor site. With the currently commercially available hyperthermia applicators, it would appear that potentially effective deep local and regional heating may be obtainable in pelvic tumors. Such treatment may be indicated as an adjunct to radiation therapy in the treatment of locally advanced or recurrent deep-seated pelvic malignancies including cancers of the colorectum, prostate, urinary bladder, and uterine cervix and corpus. As better equipment is developed, the treatment of pancreatic cancer with multi-modality regimens including hyperthermia should be considered. Hyperthermia may also be of value in the treatment of unresectable soft-tissue sarcomas and bone tumors and may be useful with preoperative radiation therapy in tumors of these sites. The roles and indications for the use of interstitial hyperthermia remain to be better defined. However, the combination of interstitial hyperthermia, and interstitial brachytherapy treatment may be valuable in the management of advanced gynecologic, head and neck, breast, and malignant brain tumors.
