N2  - Data are presented which confirm that the preoperative biochemical diagnosis of a functioning islet cell tumor is highly reliable. Accurate angiographic localization is also possible in approximately 90% of cases. The surgical approach to these tumors is thereby facilitated, permitting simple enucleation in most instances. Intraoperative monitoring of the blood glucose concentration has been most helpful, but occasionally false positive or false negative responses occur. The monitoring of immunoreactive serum insulin levels has been of no benefit to the conduct of the operation. The surgical success rate has been high, with an over all cure rate of approximately 90% and an operative mortality of 0% during the past 23 years. Approximately 10% of insulinomas are malignant; some may be amenable to curative resection. Patients with unresectable disease may be controlled symptomatically with diazoxide alone or in combination with a chemotherapeutic agent.
