N2  - The discussion of the therapeutic management of symptoms induced by hormone producing tumors of the gastrointestinal tract remains limited to insulinomas and the effect of inhibitors of immunoreactive insulin (IRI) secretion. Interest in this approach developed from the following findings: the discovery of the hyperglycemic properties of an antihypertensive nondiuretic benzothiadiazine, diazoxide; the clinical observations of the beneficial effect of diazoxide therapy in infants with idiopathic leucine sensitive hypoglycemia and patients suffering from intractable hypoglycemia due to benign or malignant islet cell tumors. Numerous case reports followed rapidly and by 1968 more than 100 patients with severe hypoglycemic symptoms had been treated with diazoxide. Diazoxide therapy has been attempted in organic and functional hypoglycemias of different etiology: in hypoglycemia connected with noninsulin producing extrapancreatic tumors; in glycogenosis type I and type III; in hypopituitarism or severe liver cell insufficiency; in hypoglycemia of infants with fetal erythroblastosis or born to diabetic mothers; in patients with postgastrectomy syndrome or essential reactive hypoglycemia. The success of these trials was limited and diazoxide has been recommended only in idiopathic hypoglycemia of children, in leucine sensitive hypoglycemia and in patients with B cell adenoma or carcinoma. In these cases, the formerly available therapeutic measures, such as glucagon, ACTH, HGH or epinephrine, are ineffective or accompanied by too many undesirable side effects compared to diazoxide. Recently, several case studies on the hyperglycemic effect of chlorpromazine, diphenylhydantoin, or mannoheptulose in insulinoma patients have been published. These compounds, including diazoxide, should be employed only during the preoperative diagnostic period or in patients with incurable hypoglycemia. Surgical removal of the insulinoma is the first and obvious choice of treatment. In cases with metastasizing B cell carcinoma the use of streptozotocin has to be considered as a second approach. Since the pharmacology and pharmacotherapy of diazoxide have been studied extensively, only this drug is discussed in detail.
